    "result_text": "There is only one test class in the project, and the full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-creation limit: creating an owner is rejected with **429 Too Many Requests** once 100 or more owners already carry today's `registrationDate`.\n\nThe logic was placed following existing conventions, each piece in its proper layer:\n\n1. **`OwnerRepository`** \u2014 added `countByRegistrationDate(LocalDate)`, mirroring the existing `countByCity` count method, with implementations in all three repositories:\n   - `SpringDataOwnerRepository` (JPQL `@Query`) \u2014 the active `spring-data-jpa` profile\n   - `JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl` (EntityManager query)\n   - `JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl` (SQL against `registration_date`)\n\n2. **`ClinicService` / `ClinicServiceImpl`** \u2014 added `countOwnersByRegistrationDate(LocalDate)`, a read-only transactional delegate alongside `countOwnersByCity`.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added a `MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY = 100` constant and a guard that returns `HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` when `countOwnersByRegistrationDate(LocalDate.now()) >= 100`. It sits after field validation/normalization (so malformed requests still get 400) and before the duplicate/quota conflict checks.\n\nOwners default their `registrationDate` to today via the existing `@PrePersist` hook, so the count reflects owners created today. The `AcceptanceTest` (create 100, then the 101st is rejected) passes, and `mvn test` reports `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
